\subsection{\code{BinomialConjugate$posterior_ess()}}{
  Effective sample sizes of the current posterior

The posterior variance is available in closed form and the credible
interval comes from the same quadrature the rest of the class uses, so
both effective sample sizes are evaluated directly. The inherited route
would instead fit a mixture to a finite sample drawn from the posterior,
which costs a mixture fit per replicate and leaves Monte Carlo error in a
quantity that has no need of it.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BinomialConjugate$posterior_ess(target_data, ...)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{target_data}}{Target study data}
      \item{\code{...}}{Unused, kept so that the simulation can call every model the
same way.}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    A list with the \code{moment} and \code{precision} effective sample sizes.
  }
}

